Subject: [PATCH 09301/11362] Make the root-liveness gap two-layer, and open
 the question of whether clicks survive windowing

The A/B's replay found the mechanism behind lp85's root inertness, and it is not dead
dynamics. Every action in its cached window is action 0 (RESET) while the planner
enumerates actions 1-5 plus clicks, so the planner's vocabulary cannot express the
window's only action. The node count closes it exactly: 37 nodes is one candidate sweep
from the root, every branch a no-op. cell_recall 1.0 is honest -- the engine models what
it saw faithfully, and what it saw is not gameplay.

That makes the cheap check the better one. Layer 1 is a static subset test, set(window
actions) against the planner vocabulary, costing zero engine calls and firing before
induction spends a token; credit to the A/B harness for proposing it. Root-liveness
becomes layer 2, for windows that pass layer 1 and might still be dynamically dead.

The entry's open question is resolved and the answer was neither branch offered. lp85's
window IS its full trajectory, so the engine was trained at the planning root; and ar25's
window IS a late slice whose engine still generalizes back to the root and plans to a real
level-up. Not a distribution mismatch -- a window-build artifact, which is the project's
own foundational ARC rule biting again.

A wider question opens in its place. The registry records lp85 as click-only [ACTION6]
with 8 levels reproduced and a 96-click sequence banked, so its real solve is clicks and
the cached window has none -- not a degraded capture, no capture. The three clean games are
all keyboard with no ACTION6 anywhere, which perfectly confounds "lp85 is special" with
"clicks do not survive window construction". The second reading would mean every
click-based game induces on degenerate transitions and fails silently at cell_recall 1.0.
Recorded as open with a one-line discriminating test; not yet run.

